// fcpwb2.bonsai with the points over the threads: WoSX's default binary
// tree in the other layout -- each interior node's row holding its two
// children's boxes and rows (layouts/fcpwb2.bonsai, 56 bytes), so that a
// visit reads one row where FCPW's layout reads the node's and its two
// children's (three rows of 32) -- the same tree, FCPW's CPU traversal
// order, and the parfor over the points bound to the pinned threads
// against WoSX's tbb::parallel_for, WoSX's side as it ships on FCPW's own
// rows. Against fcpwb-threads this measures what the layout alone is worth
// in parallel, a candidate for the optimized CPU schedule and one WoSX has
// no counterpart for; the driver copies FCPW's tree into these rows
// (fcpw_bvh2.h, WOSX_BINARY_BVH2). compare.sh runs it with the driver's
// --threads, pinned to the performance cores.
import apps/wosx/schedules/fcpwb2;

schedule {
    solve_all.bind(i, CPUThread);
}
